# Interpreter frames were one to two words too small for a method at the
# compiler's local limit. The compiler bounds a method's local space
# (mem_size, locals only) by LOCAL_SIZE, 768 bytes = 96 words, but the
# interpreter stores local id n at mem[n + 1] -- mem[0] is the instance --
# and a method that uses and/or, a ternary, a select or creates a lambda
# reserves local id 0 for a temporary, pushing its declared locals up one
# more word. Frames were calloc'd at exactly LOCAL_SIZE, so a 96-slot method
# with that temporary wrote mem[96] and mem[97] past the buffer: heap
# corruption (0xC0000374 on Windows) under --jit=off. The frame now holds
# LOCAL_SIZE plus those two header words, and LICM no longer adds a cached
# slot that would take a method past LOCAL_SIZE.
#
# Each method below declares exactly 96 local slots (parameters included, a
# lambda local counts two) and holds the temporary: WithAndOr uses '&',
# WithLambda creates a capturing lambda (its closure pointer goes through
# local id 0), and WithLoop hoists an array size under -opt s3. Each recurses
# so live frames sit next to each other, then checks every local it stored.
# Prints PASS when all values survive; exits 1 otherwise.
class LambdaAndOrEntrySpace {
